===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[hit]] with a racquet.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Clarissa, or, the History of a Young Lady|author=Samuel Richardson|year=1820|passage=You two might indeed have '''racqueted''' the ball betwixt you, as you say.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Sensation|author=Norman Keifetz|year=1975|page=21|passage=Potter drove up along the wire fence of the yard, and there on the tennis court, a kid '''racqueted''' a ball against a brick wall behind the courts.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Searching for Mary: An Exploration of Marian Apparitions Across the U.S.|author=Mark Garvey|year=1998|ISBN=0452279526|passage=One of the tennis center's satellite courts was lit to near daylight brightness by white floodlights; as I approached I could hear the steady, dry pock . . . pock of a tennis ball being '''racqueted''' back and forth.}}
# To [[play]] a [[game]] that involves using a racquet.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1948|title=Princeton Alumni Weekly - Volume 49|page=91|passage=When he isn't '''racqueting''', Brad claims he puts in the time with Alfred Dunhill of London.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1962|title=Encore - Volumes 7-9|author=Leonard Rusell|page=132|passage=...played no games as a boy, but since he did me that good turn I have '''racqueted''' about happily.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|title=First person multiple, third person femuline|author=Frances Charteris|page=89|passage=I arrived to find a tennis foursome - architect & his wife, you & my mother, '''racquetting''' through a country english afternoon just as it's been bonged since Shakespeare penned his pages.}}
# To [[dart]] about in a manner reminiscent of a ball hit by a racquet.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1938|title=Guns in the Valley|author=William Byron Mowery|page=96|passage=Flashlights in hand, they made their way down the slope and '''racqueted''' out upon the lake.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|title=Fantastically Queer Beasts and Magics|author=A.D. Padgett & Mrs. Arthur H.D. Acland|page=33|ISBN=0957291973|passage=Fancy his disgust when (having given up all hopes of watching the full moon, he had just fallen asleep) he was awakened by the noise of that rat once more '''racqueting''' about all over the room.}}
# To [[exchange]] [[back and forth]], similar to the way a tennis ball [[volley]]s back and forth.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|title=Wife to Mr Milton|author=Robert Graves|ISBN=0141970650|passage=Many of his choicest years of life were employ'd in wrangling, and receiving and '''racquetting''' back reproach, accusation and sarcasm.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|title=Totally Wired: The Wild Rise and Crazy Fall of the First Dotcom Dream|author=Andrew Smith|ISBN=1471114732|passage=More astounding still is the volume of trading, as a manic afternoon sees Netscape shares change hands 13.88 million times; an average of almost two and a half times each, with some '''racqueted''' back and forth like tennis balls . . . a curious pattern which will become familiar to dotcom stock-watchers.}}

